Begin electrical reasoning with charge and a declared system (@physics-charge-account: 0, is #chapter, #hephaestus-core "../electromagnetism/foundations/Charge-field-potential-current-and-energy.lingua#r_73F672N5XQ9B4B1MPJAKCK2EGS") { r_002E4C685D007D6227F9770A73
The earlier core connected motion, thermal behavior and waves to system boundaries, interactions and observations. Electrical reasoning uses those same disciplines while introducing a different empirical property: electric charge. Attraction or repulsion is an observation; a signed charge assignment and a force law form a model explaining a pattern of observations. Neither the word electricity nor a displayed voltage is a complete system description.

In ordinary atomic matter, electrons carry negative charge and protons positive charge of equal magnitude e. Ions have a nonzero net charge because their positive and negative contributions differ. A neutral sample can contain many charged constituents with a zero signed sum. Redistribution within it can change its interactions without changing its net charge. Attraction alone does not identify an object's net sign: a nearby charged object can polarize a neutral one. Microscopic transport, polarization and quantum descriptions need further material theory; do not treat a simple pair-force formula as a complete account of matter.

Define Q as the signed net charge inside a chosen boundary. In the processes considered here, its change equals charge entering minus charge leaving. Transferring an amount from one part to another changes their separate accounts while preserving the sum for a boundary containing both. With no charge crossing that outer boundary, Q is constant. This is charge conservation, not a deduction from mass conservation or an assertion that every charged particle remains individually unchanged. The unit is the coulomb; current later supplies coulombs per second. Existing metrology supplies current SI definitions when numerical constants are needed.

For example, an initially neutral pair of objects can acquire charges +q and −q by transfer while its combined charge remains zero. The objects can nevertheless interact and the separated state can store energy. A conducting object allows charge redistribution on some time scale; an insulating description restricts that transport within a stated regime. Both are material models, not declarations that an insulator contains no charge or that a conductor has no electric field in every possible situation. Electrostatic equilibrium and a conductor carrying current are different states.

Prepare a table whose columns are system boundary, net charge, separated charges, motion, force evidence and energy changes. Include a neutral pair, a charged probe near a neutral object, and two separated charged objects. Identify what was observed, what was inferred, and which extra measurement would challenge the inference. No rubbing, spark or material procedure is needed to reason through this table. The exercises here concern stated models and existing evidence rather than a performed experiment.

**Reading and work:** [University Physics Volume 2 — OpenStax](../electromagnetism/sources/University-Physics-Volume-2.lingua#r_5JQ0CPQ0RNJNVH175Y9V305J5Q) section 5.1 provides introductory charge observations and accounting. Read its material examples critically: the common block uses ordinary electron/ion charge transfer and local conservation, without adopting every universal statement about particle structure or mechanisms of charging. Develop a signed transfer ledger for three parts and prove cancellation of all internal transfers. Explain why net zero charge does not imply zero field at every surrounding point. Then use [Charge, field, potential, current and energy are different quantities](../electromagnetism/foundations/Charge-field-potential-current-and-energy.lingua#r_73F672N5XQ9B4B1MPJAKCK2EGS) to keep the new quantities distinct before writing a field equation.

**Continue:** [Charge, field, potential, current and energy are different quantities](../electromagnetism/foundations/Charge-field-potential-current-and-energy.lingua#r_73F672N5XQ9B4B1MPJAKCK2EGS).

